ed right now the spore has  its own dna and a thick coat  the thick coat is actually formed up of  peptidoglycan and this is known as  cortex  eventually this cortex would dehydrate  so water moves out of it  and ultimately  the dipelonic acid and thick peptidon  core form a heat resistant  spore wall  eventually this pore wall  or this particular spore can burst out  from the vegetative cell  and can survive or withstand several uh  harsh environmental situations  now let's look at  how spore is really resistant to so much  environmental uh  situations  actually endospores are resistant to  antibiotic disinfectants physical agents  as well  so let's look at why endospores are so  much resistant  calcium dipole in it which is a  conjugation of dipiccolonic acid with  calcium helps to stabilize and protect  the endospore dna  small acid-soluble proteins such as  sasps  which helps  endospore dna and protect it from heat  drying or chemical radiations  eventually the cortex of the spore  remove water from the interior of the  endospore  and it kind of  helps the spore from heat mediated  damages  lastly there are particular enzymes  within the spore which are known as  which are actually class of dna repair  enzymes that helps  if there is any kind of dna damage or  not so all of these factors collectively  make the spore resistant to so much of  environmental  harsh situations  now this is really important in terms of  clinical setting let's say there would  be an endoscopy where a probe would be  inserted into your elementary canal  and in this situation if there is some  amount of bacterial spore on these  probes then it can actually invade your  elementary canal and  this pores can germinate there in simple  terms it can infect you so that is why  in hospital settings these pores  has to be killed with the help of proper  uh disinfectant reagent such as  ethylene oxide  now bacillus anthracis which can cause  anthrax can also form spores so from a  clinical point 